  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">1st Phorm Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Level-1, Phormula-1, the Natural versions and Vegan Power Pro, checked against 1st Phorm's own nutrition panels, the NSF Certified for Sport database and the price per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#level-1">What Is in Level-1?</a>
      <a href="#phormula-1">What Is in Phormula-1?</a>
      <a href="#natural-vegan">Natural and Vegan Versions</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g and Amazon</a>
      <a href="#taste">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">1st Phorm vs ON and Dymatize</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          1st Phorm protein powder is well made and unusually well tested, but it is expensive: Level-1 and Phormula-1 both give 23 g of protein per scoop in most flavors and cost about $2.28 and $2.54 per 25 g at 1st Phorm's list prices. The flavor you pick changes the sweetener and the certification. Below: labels, testing, cost, Amazon availability and cheaper swaps.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 1st Phorm Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            1st Phorm protein powder is a good product at a high price. Level-1 and Phormula-1 both give 23 g of protein per scoop in their main flavors, per 1st Phorm's nutrition panels at the time of writing, and many flavors of both are NSF Certified for Sport, which is rarer than most buyers realise. The catch is cost: about $2.28 per 25 g of protein for Level-1 and $2.54 for Phormula-1 at 1stphorm.com list prices, against about $1.49 for <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard</a> on Amazon.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete who needs an NSF-certified flavor, you like 1st Phorm's dessert flavors, or you want a whey and milk protein blend from one brand with a 110% refund guarantee. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you mainly want the cheapest 25 g of protein, you avoid sucralose (unless you pick a Natural version), or you want to buy on Amazon, where we found no 1st Phorm powders sold by the brand.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Level-1 (Milk Chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey + milk protein concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g in 33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.28</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Level-1 Natural (Chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate + milk protein isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g in 34 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.19</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Phormula-1 (Chocolate Milkshake)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey protein isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g in 29 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.54</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Phormula-1 Natural (Chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey protein isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g in 29 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia (Reb A)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.54</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vegan Power Pro</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pea + organic rice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g in 28 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia + monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.50</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            All figures above come from the nutrition panels on 1stphorm.com when we checked in November 2026, and the cost column uses the list price of a 30-serving tub with no subscription discount. Protein and calories vary slightly by flavor, which 1st Phorm itself points out after its recent reformulation, so check the panel for the exact flavor you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 1st Phorm Protein Powders and the Closest Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            1st Phorm sells its protein powders on its own site and app, and we could not find Level-1 or Phormula-1 sold by 1st Phorm on Amazon (only its protein sticks, meat sticks and bars). The first two cards are 1st Phorm's own powders, priced at 1stphorm.com. The rest are the closest Amazon alternatives, each with a live price and our cost per 25 g. Prices change, so check the listing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="level-1" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in 1st Phorm Level-1 Protein Powder, and Does It Have Casein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1st Phorm Level-1 is a blend of whey protein concentrate and milk protein concentrate, per 1st Phorm's own FAQ and the Milk Chocolate ingredient list at the time of writing. A 33 g scoop of Milk Chocolate gives 23 g of protein, 130 calories, 2 g of fat, 3 g of carbohydrate and 2 g of sugar with 0 g added, plus 105 mg of sodium and 250 mg of calcium. The rest of the ingredient list is short: cocoa, natural flavor, and under 2% of salt, sucralose and sunflower lecithin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Level-1 does contain casein, even though the word casein is not on the label. Milk protein concentrate keeps the proteins of milk in their natural ratio, which is roughly 80% casein and 20% whey, so the milk protein half of Level-1 brings a meaningful amount of casein with it. That is the basis for 1st Phorm calling Level-1 a sustained-release protein that breaks down over two to three hours. There is no added micellar casein and no egg protein on the label we read, so if you have seen Level-1 described as a whey, casein and egg blend, that is an older or third-party description, not the current tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein density is the number to watch. 23 g of protein in a 33 g scoop is about 70% protein by weight, which is normal for a concentrate-based blend but lower than an isolate. The remaining weight is mostly carbohydrate, fat, cocoa and flavor, which is part of why buyers describe Level-1 as thicker and creamier than an isolate. 1st Phorm also advertises 5 g of naturally occurring BCAAs and 2 g of glutamine per scoop, and those are amounts that come with the protein itself rather than added extras.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener depends on the flavor. The standard flavors we checked use sucralose, while Chocolate Natural and Vanilla Natural use monk fruit instead, per 1st Phorm. The brand says it recently removed all artificial dyes and flavors from Level-1 without changing the protein, which still lands at 23-24 g per scoop depending on flavor. Every flavor contains milk, Level-1 is labelled gluten-free, and Salted Peanut Butter contains peanuts. 1st Phorm notes that Level-1 is not low in lactose, so anyone sensitive to lactose should look at Phormula-1 instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">LABEL CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Level-1 is whey plus milk protein, not a four-protein blend</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Current Level-1 panels list whey protein concentrate and milk protein concentrate as the protein sources. The casein comes from the milk protein. No egg, no added micellar casein and no isolate are listed in the Milk Chocolate flavor we read.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="phormula-1" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in 1st Phorm Phormula-1, and Is It Hydrolyzed?
          </h2>

          <p>
            1st Phorm Phormula-1 is a whey protein isolate, and it is not hydrolyzed. The Chocolate Milkshake panel at the time of writing lists whey protein isolate, cocoa, sunflower lecithin, natural flavor, salt and sucralose, and a 29 g scoop gives 23 g of protein, 110 calories, 0.5 g of fat, 1 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar. 1st Phorm describes the isolate as low-temperature processed and cross-flow microfiltered to remove most fat and lactose without chemicals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plenty of buyers search for Phormula-1 as a hydrolyzed whey, and the brand's fast-absorbing language encourages that idea, but the ingredient list does not include hydrolyzed whey or whey peptides. That matters only if you specifically want a hydrolysate: research has not shown that hydrolyzed whey builds more muscle than ordinary isolate for most people, and isolate already digests quickly. If you do want one, <a href="/best-hydrolyzed-whey-protein/">our hydrolyzed whey guide</a> covers the options, and Dymatize ISO100 is the nearest Amazon product with a hydrolyzed fraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Phormula-1 is leaner than Level-1. 23 g of protein in 29 g of powder is about 79% protein by weight, against about 70% for Level-1, and the isolate carries almost no sugar or fat. 1st Phorm recommends mixing one scoop with six ounces of ice-cold water, shaking or stirring rather than blending, and letting it sit for 60 seconds so the foam settles. It says Phormula-1 is best taken with water right after training, often stacked with its Ignition carbohydrate drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Phormula-1 contains only small traces of lactose, per 1st Phorm, but it is not lactose-free, vegan or dairy-free. The brand points lactose-intolerant buyers to Phormula-1 Clear, a clear whey isolate it describes as 100% lactose-free. Like Level-1, Phormula-1 is labelled gluten-free, every flavor contains milk, and most flavors are produced in a facility that also processes tree nuts, peanuts and wheat, so anyone with a serious allergy should read the label on the specific tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our verdict on the formula is positive: Phormula-1 is a clean, simple isolate with nothing on the label that needs explaining. The question is only whether its price, which we cover below, is worth it next to isolates of the same quality on Amazon. For how isolate differs from concentrate in practice, see <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="natural-vegan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What About Level-1 Natural, Phormula-1 Natural and Vegan Power Pro?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Level-1 Natural and Phormula-1 Natural are the same protein bases with natural sweeteners instead of sucralose, and Vegan Power Pro is 1st Phorm's plant protein. Per 1st Phorm, the only difference between Level-1 and Level-1 Natural is the sweetener, and Phormula-1 Natural differs from Phormula-1 only in its sweeteners and its shorter flavor list. All three cost the same as or less than their standard versions at list price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Level-1 Natural</h3>

          <p>
            Level-1 Natural comes in Chocolate and Vanilla. Per the Chocolate panel at the time of writing, a 34 g scoop gives 24 g of protein and 130 calories, with 3 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar. The ingredients are whey protein concentrate, milk protein isolate, alkalized cocoa, natural flavors, sunflower lecithin, sea salt and monk fruit extract. At $62.99 for 30 servings, it works out to about $2.19 per 25 g, slightly cheaper per gram than standard Level-1 because of the extra gram of protein. Level-1 Natural does not appear in the NSF Certified for Sport database we searched.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Phormula-1 Natural</h3>

          <p>
            Phormula-1 Natural comes in Chocolate, Strawberry and Vanilla, and all three appear in the NSF Certified for Sport database. The Chocolate panel lists 23 g of protein in a 29 g scoop with 100 calories and 0 g of sugar, sweetened with rebaudioside A, a purified stevia extract. 1st Phorm's mixing directions say one scoop yields 21 g of protein, so the figure may differ by flavor; check the panel on your tub. At $69.99 for 30 servings, Chocolate costs about $2.54 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vegan Power Pro</h3>

          <p>
            1st Phorm's dairy-free option is Vegan Power Pro, a pea and organic rice protein blend. Per the Chocolate Mint Cookie panel, a 28 g scoop gives 19 g of protein and 110 calories, with added vitamins at 10% of the daily value and a fruit and vegetable extract blend, sweetened with stevia leaf extract and monk fruit. At $56.99 for 30 servings it costs about $2.50 per 25 g, which is expensive for plant protein. We did not find it in the NSF database. For cheaper dairy-free options, see <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">our plant protein guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is 1st Phorm Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and better than most brands: selected flavors of Level-1, Phormula-1 and Phormula-1 Natural are NSF Certified for Sport, per the NSF Certified for Sport database when we searched it in November 2026. NSF lists Level-1 in flavors including Milk Chocolate, Vanilla Ice Cream, Strawberry Milkshake, Blueberry Muffin, Caramel Latte, Ice Cream Cookie and Salted Peanut Butter, which matches the seven flavors 1st Phorm names on its own NSF page.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Phormula-1, the NSF database lists Chocolate Milkshake, Vanilla Milkshake, CTC, Chocolate Mint Cookie, Loop D Fruit, Orange Dreamsicle and Root Beer Float among others, plus Natural Chocolate, Natural Strawberry and Natural Vanilla. Several Phormula-1 Clear flavors are listed too. The NSF database also still shows some flavors that are no longer on 1st Phorm's flavor menu, so match the exact flavor and look for the NSF mark on the tub rather than assuming the whole line is covered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every flavor is certified. 1st Phorm says plainly on its NSF page that not all of its supplements are NSF certified, and on the Level-1 product page flavors such as Birthday Cake, Maple Butter Waffle, Iced Oatmeal Cookie and Pumpkin Spice Latte do not appear on the brand's NSF list. For uncertified products, 1st Phorm says everything is made in an SQF-certified, FDA-inspected facility and tested by independent third-party labs, and its product pages also display an Informed Choice Trusted badge. Those claims come from the brand; the NSF listing is the one you can verify yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why it matters: NSF Certified for Sport screens for more than 270 substances banned in sport, checks that the label matches what is in the tub, and audits the facility, which is why leagues such as MLB and the NHL point athletes to it. If you are tested by the NCAA, a pro league or USADA, pick a flavor that appears in the NSF database on the day you buy. If you are not tested, certification is still a useful signal of label accuracy, but it is one reason among several to pay 1st Phorm's premium, not a must-have. Our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers heavy metals and testing in more detail.
          </p>

